{
  "video": {
    "id": "3edzxv_mYZk",
    "title": "Proteins, Levels of Structure, Non-Covalent Forces, Excerpt 1 | MIT 7.01SC Fundamentals of Biology",
    "url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3edzxv_mYZk",
    "channel": "MIT OpenCourseWare",
    "duration": 1135,
    "language": "en"
  },
  "segment_count": 246,
  "segments": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "start": 10.538,
      "end": 12.96,
      "duration": 2.422,
      "text": "HAZEL SIVE: All right."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "start": 12.96,
      "end": 20.83,
      "duration": 7.87,
      "text": "Let's move on to the second topic of our discussion today,"
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "start": 20.83,
      "end": 25.5,
      "duration": 4.67,
      "text": "which we will start today and then continue on Friday."
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "start": 25.5,
      "end": 29.58,
      "duration": 4.08,
      "text": "And this is a discussion of the proteins."
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "start": 29.58,
      "end": 32.97,
      "duration": 3.39,
      "text": "The proteins, probably the most fascinating class of"
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "start": 32.97,
      "end": 46.18,
      "duration": 13.21,
      "text": "macromolecules, 55% of the dry mass of a cell, and proteins"
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "start": 46.18,
      "end": 48.32,
      "duration": 2.14,
      "text": "function everywhere."
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "start": 48.32,
      "end": 49.835,
      "duration": 1.515,
      "text": "They do almost everything."
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "start": 54.07,
      "end": 57.47,
      "duration": 3.4,
      "text": "Strictly speaking, proteins are not hereditary"
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "start": 57.47,
      "end": 60.91,
      "duration": 3.44,
      "text": "information, although Professor Jacks will talk with"
    },
    {
      "index": 10,
      "start": 60.91,
      "end": 65.67,
      "duration": 4.76,
      "text": "you about a class of proteins called prions, which kind of"
    },
    {
      "index": 11,
      "start": 65.67,
      "end": 71.18,
      "duration": 5.51,
      "text": "are hereditary information."
    },
    {
      "index": 12,
      "start": 71.18,
      "end": 77.81,
      "duration": 6.63,
      "text": "So not hereditary info, but they do"
    },
    {
      "index": 13,
      "start": 77.81,
      "end": 80.7,
      "duration": 2.89,
      "text": "almost everything else."
    },
    {
      "index": 14,
      "start": 80.7,
      "end": 83.155,
      "duration": 2.455,
      "text": "They form the structure of the cell."
    },
    {
      "index": 15,
      "start": 87.59,
      "end": 92.52,
      "duration": 4.93,
      "text": "They form a major class of catalysts called enzymes that"
    },
    {
      "index": 16,
      "start": 92.52,
      "end": 96.06,
      "duration": 3.54,
      "text": "we will talk about on Friday."
    },
    {
      "index": 17,
      "start": 96.06,
      "end": 100.05,
      "duration": 3.99,
      "text": "They function in defense as in the immune system."
    },
    {
      "index": 18,
      "start": 100.05,
      "end": 107.04,
      "duration": 6.99,
      "text": "They allow cells to move, dot, dot, dot."
    },
    {
      "index": 19,
      "start": 107.04,
      "end": 107.58,
      "duration": 0.54,
      "text": "Okay?"
    },
    {
      "index": 20,
      "start": 107.58,
      "end": 111.65,
      "duration": 4.07,
      "text": "We'll talk about proteins on and on and on as major players"
    },
    {
      "index": 21,
      "start": 111.65,
      "end": 114.11,
      "duration": 2.46,
      "text": "in the fabric of life."
    },
    {
      "index": 22,
      "start": 114.11,
      "end": 128.99,
      "duration": 14.88,
      "text": "Their monomer is an amino acid that is abbreviated"
    },
    {
      "index": 23,
      "start": 128.99,
      "end": 132.95,
      "duration": 3.96,
      "text": "AA or little aa."
    },
    {
      "index": 24,
      "start": 132.95,
      "end": 139.36,
      "duration": 6.41,
      "text": "And it has a particular structure that forms around a"
    },
    {
      "index": 25,
      "start": 139.36,
      "end": 144.06,
      "duration": 4.7,
      "text": "central carbon, which is called the alpha carbon."
    },
    {
      "index": 26,
      "start": 144.06,
      "end": 148.19,
      "duration": 4.13,
      "text": "And from this alpha carbon, there are four groups that"
    },
    {
      "index": 27,
      "start": 148.19,
      "end": 153.22,
      "duration": 5.03,
      "text": "emanate, something called R, which is the functional"
    },
    {
      "index": 28,
      "start": 153.22,
      "end": 158.48,
      "duration": 5.26,
      "text": "interesting group of each amino acid."
    },
    {
      "index": 29,
      "start": 158.48,
      "end": 169.4,
      "duration": 10.92,
      "text": "So R is characteristic of each class of amino acid."
    },
    {
      "index": 30,
      "start": 169.4,
      "end": 172.15,
      "duration": 2.75,
      "text": "We'll talk more about this in a moment."
    },
    {
      "index": 31,
      "start": 172.15,
      "end": 176.92,
      "duration": 4.77,
      "text": "And then we'll put in a hydrogen and then there is a"
    },
    {
      "index": 32,
      "start": 176.92,
      "end": 180.53,
      "duration": 3.61,
      "text": "nitrogen or an amine group, which I'm going to draw here"
    },
    {
      "index": 33,
      "start": 180.53,
      "end": 182.64,
      "duration": 2.11,
      "text": "as NH3 positive."
    },
    {
      "index": 34,
      "start": 182.64,
      "end": 187.18,
      "duration": 4.54,
      "text": "And the other group is a carboxyl group, which I'm"
    },
    {
      "index": 35,
      "start": 187.18,
      "end": 191.05,
      "duration": 3.87,
      "text": "going to draw here again as ionized."
    },
    {
      "index": 36,
      "start": 191.05,
      "end": 195.27,
      "duration": 4.22,
      "text": "So R, actually, I'm going to give a special name to."
    },
    {
      "index": 37,
      "start": 195.27,
      "end": 198.97,
      "duration": 3.7,
      "text": "We'll call it a side chain."
    },
    {
      "index": 38,
      "start": 198.97,
      "end": 204.62,
      "duration": 5.65,
      "text": "And this side chain can be a charged group."
    },
    {
      "index": 39,
      "start": 204.62,
      "end": 207.915,
      "duration": 3.295,
      "text": "It can be polar or non-polar."
    },
    {
      "index": 40,
      "start": 215.26,
      "end": 217.81,
      "duration": 2.55,
      "text": "And let me see what I have as the first thing."
    },
    {
      "index": 41,
      "start": 217.81,
      "end": 218.2,
      "duration": 0.39,
      "text": "Yes."
    },
    {
      "index": 42,
      "start": 218.2,
      "end": 222.13,
      "duration": 3.93,
      "text": "Here are some of the side groups in amino acids, for"
    },
    {
      "index": 43,
      "start": 222.13,
      "end": 223.75,
      "duration": 1.62,
      "text": "example, lysine."
    },
    {
      "index": 44,
      "start": 223.75,
      "end": 227.19,
      "duration": 3.44,
      "text": "Here is the alpha carbon, and here's its side group."
    },
    {
      "index": 45,
      "start": 227.19,
      "end": 229.18,
      "duration": 1.99,
      "text": "You can see it ends in an amino group."
    },
    {
      "index": 46,
      "start": 229.18,
      "end": 230.87,
      "duration": 1.69,
      "text": "It's positively charged."
    },
    {
      "index": 47,
      "start": 230.87,
      "end": 233.58,
      "duration": 2.71,
      "text": "Glutamic acid, I keep forgetting glutamic acid."
    },
    {
      "index": 48,
      "start": 233.58,
      "end": 234.59,
      "duration": 1.01,
      "text": "This is a better screen."
    },
    {
      "index": 49,
      "start": 234.59,
      "end": 236.44,
      "duration": 1.85,
      "text": "Glutamic acid ends."
    },
    {
      "index": 50,
      "start": 236.44,
      "end": 238.29,
      "duration": 1.85,
      "text": "It's got a carboxyl group at the end."
    },
    {
      "index": 51,
      "start": 238.29,
      "end": 239.155,
      "duration": 0.865,
      "text": "It's negatively charged."
    },
    {
      "index": 52,
      "start": 239.155,
      "end": 240.68,
      "duration": 1.525,
      "text": "It's an acid."
    },
    {
      "index": 53,
      "start": 240.68,
      "end": 243.94,
      "duration": 3.26,
      "text": "Here's one, tyrosine with a polar uncharged group."
    },
    {
      "index": 54,
      "start": 243.94,
      "end": 248.45,
      "duration": 4.51,
      "text": "This benzene ring and the hydroxyl gives some polarity"
    },
    {
      "index": 55,
      "start": 248.45,
      "end": 250.66,
      "duration": 2.21,
      "text": "to the molecule and here's leucine with a"
    },
    {
      "index": 56,
      "start": 250.66,
      "end": 252.46,
      "duration": 1.8,
      "text": "hydroxyl gives polarity."
    },
    {
      "index": 57,
      "start": 252.46,
      "end": 254.17,
      "duration": 1.71,
      "text": "Leucine is non-polar."
    },
    {
      "index": 58,
      "start": 254.17,
      "end": 258.16,
      "duration": 3.99,
      "text": "It's really a hydrocarbon and cystine has this interesting"
    },
    {
      "index": 59,
      "start": 258.16,
      "end": 261.76,
      "duration": 3.6,
      "text": "very reactive sulfhydryl group at the end of its R chain,"
    },
    {
      "index": 60,
      "start": 261.76,
      "end": 265.86,
      "duration": 4.1,
      "text": "which is capable of reacting with another sulfhydryl group"
    },
    {
      "index": 61,
      "start": 265.86,
      "end": 267.68,
      "duration": 1.82,
      "text": "and forming a disulfide bond."
    },
    {
      "index": 62,
      "start": 274.06,
      "end": 278.0,
      "duration": 3.94,
      "text": "The polymer, actually I think I could write it here, the"
    },
    {
      "index": 63,
      "start": 278.0,
      "end": 293.19,
      "duration": 15.19,
      "text": "polymer of amino acids looks like this, and it's called a"
    },
    {
      "index": 64,
      "start": 293.19,
      "end": 303.23,
      "duration": 10.04,
      "text": "peptide if it's less than 20 amino acids and a protein if"
    },
    {
      "index": 65,
      "start": 303.23,
      "end": 305.64,
      "duration": 2.41,
      "text": "it's about more than 20 amino acids, but"
    },
    {
      "index": 66,
      "start": 305.64,
      "end": 307.7,
      "duration": 2.06,
      "text": "that's kind of loose."
    },
    {
      "index": 67,
      "start": 307.7,
      "end": 311.4,
      "duration": 3.7,
      "text": "You should know the term peptide, polypeptide mean"
    },
    {
      "index": 68,
      "start": 311.4,
      "end": 314.44,
      "duration": 3.04,
      "text": "something a bit bigger than a peptide, protein, they're all"
    },
    {
      "index": 69,
      "start": 314.44,
      "end": 316.09,
      "duration": 1.65,
      "text": "the same chemical structure --"
    },
    {
      "index": 70,
      "start": 316.09,
      "end": 319.88,
      "duration": 3.79,
      "text": "just refers to differences in the amount of"
    },
    {
      "index": 71,
      "start": 319.88,
      "end": 321.38,
      "duration": 1.5,
      "text": "sequence in the polymer."
    },
    {
      "index": 72,
      "start": 324.43,
      "end": 328.49,
      "duration": 4.06,
      "text": "How does the protein polymer form?"
    },
    {
      "index": 73,
      "start": 328.49,
      "end": 330.34,
      "duration": 1.85,
      "text": "I'll draw that for you now."
    },
    {
      "index": 74,
      "start": 330.34,
      "end": 334.05,
      "duration": 3.71,
      "text": "And it forms by making a particular bond called a"
    },
    {
      "index": 75,
      "start": 334.05,
      "end": 338.48,
      "duration": 4.43,
      "text": "peptide bond that you should know and be able to recognize."
    },
    {
      "index": 76,
      "start": 338.48,
      "end": 343.69,
      "duration": 5.21,
      "text": "So let's draw amino acid one with carbon."
    },
    {
      "index": 77,
      "start": 343.69,
      "end": 350.1,
      "duration": 6.41,
      "text": "And we're going to put its amino group, its hydrogren,"
    },
    {
      "index": 78,
      "start": 350.1,
      "end": 354.04,
      "duration": 3.94,
      "text": "and then its carboxyl group."
    },
    {
      "index": 79,
      "start": 354.04,
      "end": 355.43,
      "duration": 1.39,
      "text": "And we're going to add to it."
    },
    {
      "index": 80,
      "start": 355.43,
      "end": 359.21,
      "duration": 3.78,
      "text": "So this is going to be amino acid one, and we're going to"
    },
    {
      "index": 81,
      "start": 359.21,
      "end": 363.915,
      "duration": 4.705,
      "text": "add to it another one with a second side chain."
    },
    {
      "index": 82,
      "start": 363.915,
      "end": 368.76,
      "duration": 4.845,
      "text": "And here, we'll draw out the hydrogens on the amino group."
    },
    {
      "index": 83,
      "start": 377.85,
      "end": 381.78,
      "duration": 3.93,
      "text": "This oxygen and the hydrogens on the amino group are going"
    },
    {
      "index": 84,
      "start": 381.78,
      "end": 389.84,
      "duration": 8.06,
      "text": "to interact, and they are going to undergo a"
    },
    {
      "index": 85,
      "start": 389.84,
      "end": 391.57,
      "duration": 1.73,
      "text": "condensation reaction."
    },
    {
      "index": 86,
      "start": 391.57,
      "end": 395.82,
      "duration": 4.25,
      "text": "And actually I realize when I drew for you the nucleotide"
    },
    {
      "index": 87,
      "start": 395.82,
      "end": 399.05,
      "duration": 3.23,
      "text": "condensation reaction, that was when we drew the"
    },
    {
      "index": 88,
      "start": 399.05,
      "end": 402.46,
      "duration": 3.41,
      "text": "dinucleotide that was forming, I realize I didn't put that"
    },
    {
      "index": 89,
      "start": 402.46,
      "end": 405.97,
      "duration": 3.51,
      "text": "there was elimination of a molecule as the dinucleotide"
    },
    {
      "index": 90,
      "start": 405.97,
      "end": 406.44,
      "duration": 0.47,
      "text": "was forming."
    },
    {
      "index": 91,
      "start": 406.44,
      "end": 408.98,
      "duration": 2.54,
      "text": "It's a bit complicated, and that was why I didn't do it."
    },
    {
      "index": 92,
      "start": 408.98,
      "end": 412.1,
      "duration": 3.12,
      "text": "But in this case, this is a kind of classic condensation"
    },
    {
      "index": 93,
      "start": 412.1,
      "end": 415.95,
      "duration": 3.85,
      "text": "reaction, which will eliminate water, and you'll end up with"
    },
    {
      "index": 94,
      "start": 415.95,
      "end": 417.55,
      "duration": 1.6,
      "text": "something that looks like this."
    },
    {
      "index": 95,
      "start": 421.03,
      "end": 422.12,
      "duration": 1.09,
      "text": "So here's your alpha."
    },
    {
      "index": 96,
      "start": 422.12,
      "end": 424.6,
      "duration": 2.48,
      "text": "I'm going to circle the alpha carbon so we don't"
    },
    {
      "index": 97,
      "start": 424.6,
      "end": 425.85,
      "duration": 1.25,
      "text": "get where they are."
    },
    {
      "index": 98,
      "start": 430.05,
      "end": 435.68,
      "duration": 5.63,
      "text": "And then we've got a carbonyl group that's joined to an"
    },
    {
      "index": 99,
      "start": 435.68,
      "end": 439.91,
      "duration": 4.23,
      "text": "amine and then the other alpha carbon, R2."
    },
    {
      "index": 100,
      "start": 445.65,
      "end": 450.6,
      "duration": 4.95,
      "text": "So here is amino acid one, amino acid two."
    },
    {
      "index": 101,
      "start": 450.6,
      "end": 456.32,
      "duration": 5.72,
      "text": "And here is a dipeptide, or diamino acid."
    },
    {
      "index": 102,
      "start": 456.32,
      "end": 459.27,
      "duration": 2.95,
      "text": "It doesn't really matter what you call it."
    },
    {
      "index": 103,
      "start": 459.27,
      "end": 466.51,
      "duration": 7.24,
      "text": "The peptide bond is this guy, and it's a very, very"
    },
    {
      "index": 104,
      "start": 466.51,
      "end": 469.725,
      "duration": 3.215,
      "text": "important bond."
    },
    {
      "index": 105,
      "start": 469.725,
      "end": 473.41,
      "duration": 3.685,
      "text": "It holds the protein chain together, and you need to be"
    },
    {
      "index": 106,
      "start": 473.41,
      "end": 475.77,
      "duration": 2.36,
      "text": "able to recognize it."
    },
    {
      "index": 107,
      "start": 475.77,
      "end": 478.69,
      "duration": 2.92,
      "text": "Two other features of this as well, which will be"
    },
    {
      "index": 108,
      "start": 478.69,
      "end": 482.89,
      "duration": 4.2,
      "text": "reminiscent of the nucleic acid case, and on one end --"
    },
    {
      "index": 109,
      "start": 482.89,
      "end": 483.89,
      "duration": 1.0,
      "text": "the ends are different."
    },
    {
      "index": 110,
      "start": 483.89,
      "end": 485.38,
      "duration": 1.49,
      "text": "Okay?"
    },
    {
      "index": 111,
      "start": 485.38,
      "end": 487.03,
      "duration": 1.65,
      "text": "The ends are different."
    },
    {
      "index": 112,
      "start": 487.03,
      "end": 496.47,
      "duration": 9.44,
      "text": "On one end, there is a free amino group, and this is"
    },
    {
      "index": 113,
      "start": 496.47,
      "end": 504.93,
      "duration": 8.46,
      "text": "called the amino end or the N-terminal of the dipeptide or"
    },
    {
      "index": 114,
      "start": 504.93,
      "end": 506.58,
      "duration": 1.65,
      "text": "of the protein."
    },
    {
      "index": 115,
      "start": 506.58,
      "end": 517.32,
      "duration": 10.74,
      "text": "And on the other end, there is a free carboxyl group, and"
    },
    {
      "index": 116,
      "start": 517.32,
      "end": 527.44,
      "duration": 10.12,
      "text": "this is termed the carboxyl or the carboxy, or the C-terminal"
    },
    {
      "index": 117,
      "start": 527.44,
      "end": 532.66,
      "duration": 5.22,
      "text": "of this dipeptide, or indeed of any protein."
    },
    {
      "index": 118,
      "start": 532.66,
      "end": 537.16,
      "duration": 4.5,
      "text": "So this is going to sound reminiscent of the case as in"
    },
    {
      "index": 119,
      "start": 537.16,
      "end": 542.46,
      "duration": 5.3,
      "text": "nucleic acids because like nucleic acids, proteins,"
    },
    {
      "index": 120,
      "start": 542.46,
      "end": 547.99,
      "duration": 5.53,
      "text": "because they have different ends, have a linear order."
    },
    {
      "index": 121,
      "start": 547.99,
      "end": 555.98,
      "duration": 7.99,
      "text": "So we'll write it again, different ends"
    },
    {
      "index": 122,
      "start": 555.98,
      "end": 558.35,
      "duration": 2.37,
      "text": "and a linear order."
    },
    {
      "index": 123,
      "start": 558.35,
      "end": 563.35,
      "duration": 5.0,
      "text": "And again, it's this linear order that can only be read in"
    },
    {
      "index": 124,
      "start": 563.35,
      "end": 567.04,
      "duration": 3.69,
      "text": "one direction or that leads to information that is not"
    },
    {
      "index": 125,
      "start": 567.04,
      "end": 572.25,
      "duration": 5.21,
      "text": "symmetric or not randomly oriented that gives the"
    },
    {
      "index": 126,
      "start": 572.25,
      "end": 574.53,
      "duration": 2.28,
      "text": "protein its particular properties."
    },
    {
      "index": 127,
      "start": 574.53,
      "end": 577.37,
      "duration": 2.84,
      "text": "So let's write this out again, formally."
    },
    {
      "index": 128,
      "start": 577.37,
      "end": 581.77,
      "duration": 4.4,
      "text": "On one end, there's the amino end."
    },
    {
      "index": 129,
      "start": 581.77,
      "end": 584.9,
      "duration": 3.13,
      "text": "You can also write this as NH2."
    },
    {
      "index": 130,
      "start": 584.9,
      "end": 586.59,
      "duration": 1.69,
      "text": "It's done kind of casually."
    },
    {
      "index": 131,
      "start": 586.59,
      "end": 591.27,
      "duration": 4.68,
      "text": "You can write it as N. Doesn't really matter to us."
    },
    {
      "index": 132,
      "start": 591.27,
      "end": 595.71,
      "duration": 4.44,
      "text": "And then here's your polymer of amino acids."
    },
    {
      "index": 133,
      "start": 595.71,
      "end": 598.06,
      "duration": 2.35,
      "text": "And here is your carboxy end."
    },
    {
      "index": 134,
      "start": 598.06,
      "end": 599.47,
      "duration": 1.41,
      "text": "You can write COO."
    },
    {
      "index": 135,
      "start": 599.47,
      "end": 601.57,
      "duration": 2.1,
      "text": "I always write COOH."
    },
    {
      "index": 136,
      "start": 601.57,
      "end": 605.46,
      "duration": 3.89,
      "text": "You can also write C. All of those are kind of given as"
    },
    {
      "index": 137,
      "start": 605.46,
      "end": 611.25,
      "duration": 5.79,
      "text": "being equivalent and this is your amino end and your"
    },
    {
      "index": 138,
      "start": 611.25,
      "end": 614.58,
      "duration": 3.33,
      "text": "carboxy end."
    },
    {
      "index": 139,
      "start": 614.58,
      "end": 618.68,
      "duration": 4.1,
      "text": "That's terminology, but you can tell from where these free"
    },
    {
      "index": 140,
      "start": 618.68,
      "end": 622.72,
      "duration": 4.04,
      "text": "chemical groups are which amino acid was added first and"
    },
    {
      "index": 141,
      "start": 622.72,
      "end": 624.13,
      "duration": 1.41,
      "text": "which was added last."
    },
    {
      "index": 142,
      "start": 624.13,
      "end": 629.06,
      "duration": 4.93,
      "text": "And the amino acid nearest the end terminal is added first."
    },
    {
      "index": 143,
      "start": 632.38,
      "end": 637.02,
      "duration": 4.64,
      "text": "The one nearest the free carboxy is the most recently"
    },
    {
      "index": 144,
      "start": 637.02,
      "end": 638.47,
      "duration": 1.45,
      "text": "added, always added last."
    },
    {
      "index": 145,
      "start": 641.81,
      "end": 646.2,
      "duration": 4.39,
      "text": "Now, we went through a bit of a calculation for nucleic"
    },
    {
      "index": 146,
      "start": 646.2,
      "end": 650.18,
      "duration": 3.98,
      "text": "acids where there are four bases, and I pointed out to"
    },
    {
      "index": 147,
      "start": 650.18,
      "end": 653.2,
      "duration": 3.02,
      "text": "you that you could get a lot of combinatorics out of four"
    },
    {
      "index": 148,
      "start": 653.2,
      "end": 656.62,
      "duration": 3.42,
      "text": "bases if you had a polymer that was long enough."
    },
    {
      "index": 149,
      "start": 656.62,
      "end": 660.08,
      "duration": 3.46,
      "text": "For proteins, the situation is actually"
    },
    {
      "index": 150,
      "start": 660.08,
      "end": 662.81,
      "duration": 2.73,
      "text": "dauntingly more complex."
    },
    {
      "index": 151,
      "start": 662.81,
      "end": 666.565,
      "duration": 3.755,
      "text": "There are 20 different amino acids."
    },
    {
      "index": 152,
      "start": 673.75,
      "end": 684.47,
      "duration": 10.72,
      "text": "So for a peptide, that's three amino acids, you would get 20"
    },
    {
      "index": 153,
      "start": 684.47,
      "end": 694.76,
      "duration": 10.29,
      "text": "to the third combinations or 8,000."
    },
    {
      "index": 154,
      "start": 694.76,
      "end": 705.06,
      "duration": 10.3,
      "text": "Proteins can be greater than 1,000 amino acids in length."
    },
    {
      "index": 155,
      "start": 705.06,
      "end": 711.81,
      "duration": 6.75,
      "text": "Proteins or peptides for little ones can range from two"
    },
    {
      "index": 156,
      "start": 711.81,
      "end": 716.96,
      "duration": 5.15,
      "text": "to greater than 1,000 amino acids."
    },
    {
      "index": 157,
      "start": 716.96,
      "end": 719.77,
      "duration": 2.81,
      "text": "And so the number of combinations of information"
    },
    {
      "index": 158,
      "start": 719.77,
      "end": 723.3,
      "duration": 3.53,
      "text": "that you can get in proteins is enormous, and that means"
    },
    {
      "index": 159,
      "start": 723.3,
      "end": 728.05,
      "duration": 4.75,
      "text": "that most of protein space has not been explored by life and"
    },
    {
      "index": 160,
      "start": 728.05,
      "end": 730.47,
      "duration": 2.42,
      "text": "can be explored in the laboratory."
    },
    {
      "index": 161,
      "start": 730.47,
      "end": 733.7,
      "duration": 3.23,
      "text": "And that's very exciting in thinking about the various"
    },
    {
      "index": 162,
      "start": 733.7,
      "end": 738.84,
      "duration": 5.14,
      "text": "functions that one can find that have not yet been found."
    },
    {
      "index": 163,
      "start": 738.84,
      "end": 742.87,
      "duration": 4.03,
      "text": "Now, the thing that is different about nucleic acids"
    },
    {
      "index": 164,
      "start": 742.87,
      "end": 745.67,
      "duration": 2.8,
      "text": "and proteins apart from their fundamental chemical"
    },
    {
      "index": 165,
      "start": 745.67,
      "end": 750.59,
      "duration": 4.92,
      "text": "structure, is that nucleic acids, as we will discuss, are"
    },
    {
      "index": 166,
      "start": 750.59,
      "end": 754.0,
      "duration": 3.41,
      "text": "used as a linear more or less, they are"
    },
    {
      "index": 167,
      "start": 754.0,
      "end": 755.93,
      "duration": 1.93,
      "text": "read as a linear string."
    },
    {
      "index": 168,
      "start": 755.93,
      "end": 759.74,
      "duration": 3.81,
      "text": "You start somewhere, and you read along the nucleic acid,"
    },
    {
      "index": 169,
      "start": 759.74,
      "end": 762.7,
      "duration": 2.96,
      "text": "and you get your information out, and that gives you some"
    },
    {
      "index": 170,
      "start": 762.7,
      "end": 764.83,
      "duration": 2.13,
      "text": "kind of a next step."
    },
    {
      "index": 171,
      "start": 764.83,
      "end": 769.82,
      "duration": 4.99,
      "text": "Proteins, although they have a linear order, are not used in"
    },
    {
      "index": 172,
      "start": 769.82,
      "end": 772.99,
      "duration": 3.17,
      "text": "this kind of straight linear way."
    },
    {
      "index": 173,
      "start": 772.99,
      "end": 776.49,
      "duration": 3.5,
      "text": "They are read once they have folded up into"
    },
    {
      "index": 174,
      "start": 776.49,
      "end": 780.65,
      "duration": 4.16,
      "text": "three-dimensional structures, and the folding of proteins"
    },
    {
      "index": 175,
      "start": 780.65,
      "end": 785.86,
      "duration": 5.21,
      "text": "into these 3D structures is intrinsic and essential for"
    },
    {
      "index": 176,
      "start": 785.86,
      "end": 786.171,
      "duration": 0.311,
      "text": "their function."
    },
    {
      "index": 177,
      "start": 786.171,
      "end": 787.421,
      "duration": 1.25,
      "text": "So protein folding --"
    },
    {
      "index": 178,
      "start": 794.15,
      "end": 796.91,
      "duration": 2.76,
      "text": "Actually, let me before we do protein folding, I realize"
    },
    {
      "index": 179,
      "start": 796.91,
      "end": 799.58,
      "duration": 2.67,
      "text": "I've been remiss in giving you these slides."
    },
    {
      "index": 180,
      "start": 799.58,
      "end": 802.47,
      "duration": 2.89,
      "text": "Here is something I drew for you on amino acid"
    },
    {
      "index": 181,
      "start": 802.47,
      "end": 805.643,
      "duration": 3.173,
      "text": "polymerization from your book, and here we are"
    },
    {
      "index": 182,
      "start": 805.643,
      "end": 807.3,
      "duration": 1.657,
      "text": "where we should be."
    },
    {
      "index": 183,
      "start": 807.3,
      "end": 812.32,
      "duration": 5.02,
      "text": "Protein folding is required for function."
    },
    {
      "index": 184,
      "start": 821.66,
      "end": 826.58,
      "duration": 4.92,
      "text": "The linear order of amino acids in the first peptide"
    },
    {
      "index": 185,
      "start": 826.58,
      "end": 830.17,
      "duration": 3.59,
      "text": "chain that is synthesized is called the primary structure."
    },
    {
      "index": 186,
      "start": 837.06,
      "end": 841.38,
      "duration": 4.32,
      "text": "So primary structure refers to the linear"
    },
    {
      "index": 187,
      "start": 841.38,
      "end": 845.65,
      "duration": 4.27,
      "text": "order of amino acids."
    },
    {
      "index": 188,
      "start": 851.25,
      "end": 854.16,
      "duration": 2.91,
      "text": "And these amino acids, of course, are held together by"
    },
    {
      "index": 189,
      "start": 854.16,
      "end": 860.69,
      "duration": 6.53,
      "text": "covalent bonds as in peptide bonds."
    },
    {
      "index": 190,
      "start": 860.69,
      "end": 864.22,
      "duration": 3.53,
      "text": "But this primary structure, while absolutely essential for"
    },
    {
      "index": 191,
      "start": 864.22,
      "end": 869.0,
      "duration": 4.78,
      "text": "protein function, is not the functional protein."
    },
    {
      "index": 192,
      "start": 869.0,
      "end": 872.88,
      "duration": 3.88,
      "text": "It folds up, this primary structure, this linear chain,"
    },
    {
      "index": 193,
      "start": 872.88,
      "end": 876.78,
      "duration": 3.9,
      "text": "folds up and folds again and folds again."
    },
    {
      "index": 194,
      "start": 876.78,
      "end": 880.85,
      "duration": 4.07,
      "text": "The way I think of it is, if you took a piece of string or"
    },
    {
      "index": 195,
      "start": 880.85,
      "end": 884.58,
      "duration": 3.73,
      "text": "an old-fashioned telephone cord, and you folded it up,"
    },
    {
      "index": 196,
      "start": 884.58,
      "end": 890.11,
      "duration": 5.53,
      "text": "you start rolling it up into a roll, it will roll up, and"
    },
    {
      "index": 197,
      "start": 890.11,
      "end": 893.81,
      "duration": 3.7,
      "text": "it'll fold back on itself, and fold back on itself, and fold"
    },
    {
      "index": 198,
      "start": 893.81,
      "end": 895.17,
      "duration": 1.36,
      "text": "back on itself."
    },
    {
      "index": 199,
      "start": 895.17,
      "end": 897.77,
      "duration": 2.6,
      "text": "And that's kind of the deal with proteins."
    },
    {
      "index": 200,
      "start": 897.77,
      "end": 900.54,
      "duration": 2.77,
      "text": "So we have to consider something called secondary"
    },
    {
      "index": 201,
      "start": 900.54,
      "end": 910.75,
      "duration": 10.21,
      "text": "structure where the linear chain of amino acids folds"
    },
    {
      "index": 202,
      "start": 910.75,
      "end": 912.31,
      "duration": 1.56,
      "text": "back on itself."
    },
    {
      "index": 203,
      "start": 912.31,
      "end": 923.15,
      "duration": 10.84,
      "text": "So the linear amino acid chain folds up, and it"
    },
    {
      "index": 204,
      "start": 923.15,
      "end": 926.06,
      "duration": 2.91,
      "text": "does so in two ways."
    },
    {
      "index": 205,
      "start": 926.06,
      "end": 932.81,
      "duration": 6.75,
      "text": "It forms something called an alpha helix or it forms"
    },
    {
      "index": 206,
      "start": 932.81,
      "end": 937.42,
      "duration": 4.61,
      "text": "something called a beta sheet or a beta pleated sheet, and"
    },
    {
      "index": 207,
      "start": 937.42,
      "end": 940.48,
      "duration": 3.06,
      "text": "these are characteristics folding structure or folding"
    },
    {
      "index": 208,
      "start": 940.48,
      "end": 944.36,
      "duration": 3.88,
      "text": "patterns that are governed by hydrogen bonds."
    },
    {
      "index": 209,
      "start": 946.93,
      "end": 950.86,
      "duration": 3.93,
      "text": "But that secondary structure of the protein is not the"
    },
    {
      "index": 210,
      "start": 950.86,
      "end": 954.05,
      "duration": 3.19,
      "text": "functional protein."
    },
    {
      "index": 211,
      "start": 954.05,
      "end": 959.18,
      "duration": 5.13,
      "text": "Those folded hydrogen-bonded alpha helices or beta sheets"
    },
    {
      "index": 212,
      "start": 959.18,
      "end": 966.91,
      "duration": 7.73,
      "text": "fold on themselves again to form the tertiary structure of"
    },
    {
      "index": 213,
      "start": 966.91,
      "end": 968.27,
      "duration": 1.36,
      "text": "the protein."
    },
    {
      "index": 214,
      "start": 968.27,
      "end": 976.9,
      "duration": 8.63,
      "text": "So this is more folding of the alpha"
    },
    {
      "index": 215,
      "start": 976.9,
      "end": 984.27,
      "duration": 7.37,
      "text": "helices or the beta sheets."
    },
    {
      "index": 216,
      "start": 984.27,
      "end": 988.06,
      "duration": 3.79,
      "text": "And this tertiary structure can involve any number of"
    },
    {
      "index": 217,
      "start": 988.06,
      "end": 989.84,
      "duration": 1.78,
      "text": "different kinds of bonds."
    },
    {
      "index": 218,
      "start": 989.84,
      "end": 994.8,
      "duration": 4.96,
      "text": "It can involve covalent bonds like sulfhydryl disulfide"
    },
    {
      "index": 219,
      "start": 994.8,
      "end": 1002.33,
      "duration": 7.53,
      "text": "bonds, hydrogen bonds, hydrophobic bonds, and all of"
    },
    {
      "index": 220,
      "start": 1002.33,
      "end": 1004.96,
      "duration": 2.63,
      "text": "these things give a tertiary structure."
    },
    {
      "index": 221,
      "start": 1004.96,
      "end": 1009.72,
      "duration": 4.76,
      "text": "All of them use one linear polypeptide or protein chain."
    },
    {
      "index": 222,
      "start": 1009.72,
      "end": 1012.61,
      "duration": 2.89,
      "text": "But then there's a fourth folding that is required for"
    },
    {
      "index": 223,
      "start": 1012.61,
      "end": 1014.72,
      "duration": 2.11,
      "text": "the function of many proteins."
    },
    {
      "index": 224,
      "start": 1014.72,
      "end": 1022.67,
      "duration": 7.95,
      "text": "This is the quaternary structure, and this refers to,"
    },
    {
      "index": 225,
      "start": 1022.67,
      "end": 1031.504,
      "duration": 8.834,
      "text": "so again, this is of the primary and secondary chain."
    },
    {
      "index": 226,
      "start": 1038.15,
      "end": 1042.27,
      "duration": 4.12,
      "text": "The quaternary structure is an association between two"
    },
    {
      "index": 227,
      "start": 1042.27,
      "end": 1047.02,
      "duration": 4.75,
      "text": "different proteins in a non-covalent way, usually."
    },
    {
      "index": 228,
      "start": 1047.02,
      "end": 1066.37,
      "duration": 19.35,
      "text": "So this is association between two different protein chains"
    },
    {
      "index": 229,
      "start": 1066.37,
      "end": 1071.3,
      "duration": 4.93,
      "text": "and two different protein chains that are in some kind"
    },
    {
      "index": 230,
      "start": 1071.3,
      "end": 1074.23,
      "duration": 2.93,
      "text": "of tertiary structure."
    },
    {
      "index": 231,
      "start": 1074.23,
      "end": 1077.52,
      "duration": 3.29,
      "text": "And usually this association is non-covalent."
    },
    {
      "index": 232,
      "start": 1082.3,
      "end": 1088.45,
      "duration": 6.15,
      "text": "The way protein structure is governed and proteins fold is"
    },
    {
      "index": 233,
      "start": 1088.45,
      "end": 1091.48,
      "duration": 3.03,
      "text": "mysterious, and very complicated, and not well"
    },
    {
      "index": 234,
      "start": 1091.48,
      "end": 1092.6,
      "duration": 1.12,
      "text": "understood."
    },
    {
      "index": 235,
      "start": 1092.6,
      "end": 1093.35,
      "duration": 0.75,
      "text": "And there we go."
    },
    {
      "index": 236,
      "start": 1093.35,
      "end": 1096.0,
      "duration": 2.65,
      "text": "Look at this interesting stuff you can see on my screen."
    },
    {
      "index": 237,
      "start": 1096.0,
      "end": 1097.14,
      "duration": 1.14,
      "text": "Here we go."
    },
    {
      "index": 238,
      "start": 1097.14,
      "end": 1099.37,
      "duration": 2.23,
      "text": "Here, not from your book because I think it's better"
    },
    {
      "index": 239,
      "start": 1099.37,
      "end": 1103.49,
      "duration": 4.12,
      "text": "from a different book, is a picture of an alpha helix, a"
    },
    {
      "index": 240,
      "start": 1103.49,
      "end": 1111.33,
      "duration": 7.84,
      "text": "beta sheet, tertiary structures of folded"
    },
    {
      "index": 241,
      "start": 1111.33,
      "end": 1113.29,
      "duration": 1.96,
      "text": "polypeptide chains."
    },
    {
      "index": 242,
      "start": 1113.29,
      "end": 1115.65,
      "duration": 2.36,
      "text": "And here is a quaternary structure."
    },
    {
      "index": 243,
      "start": 1115.65,
      "end": 1118.93,
      "duration": 3.28,
      "text": "And as we'll talk about on Friday, it's the quaternary"
    },
    {
      "index": 244,
      "start": 1118.93,
      "end": 1121.89,
      "duration": 2.96,
      "text": "structures that are these functional units called"
    },
    {
      "index": 245,
      "start": 1121.89,
      "end": 1123.37,
      "duration": 1.48,
      "text": "enzymes, and we'll stop there."
    }
  ],
  "course": {
    "category": "Biology & Chemistry",
    "title": "Introduction to Biology",
    "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLF83B8D8C87426E44"
  }
}